nd truncated ('the recommendation is to “'). A frame difference cannot show whether any differentiated consumer proposition is viable at all, so the test cannot properly separate the theories or inform the investment decision."},"revision":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A19","A20"],"reason":"A19 contradicts itself. It is labelled 'T1 kept', yet says T1 'is not supported' and replaces the claim with T2's wording. It also stretches simulated wear-frequency evidence to cover T1's willingness-to-pay and purchase predictions. A20 then repeats the pre-update recommendation unchanged, with T1 still 'likely'. The update is noted but the response is not sound."},"recommendation":{"status":"scored","score":1,"event_ids":["A18","A20"],"reason":"A20 commits to a limited launch while also calling for the test first. It rates T1 'likely' even though every T1 assessment was neutral (A11) and the update found T1 unsupported (A19). It assumes an 'existing Hellberg ... distribution network' reaches consumers, which no source shows. It adds musicians as a target without basis and borrows 'peace of mind' from a head-protection company claim. Reversal conditions are given but partly truncated and illogical: higher-than-expected pilot sales would shift the recommendation toward medical messaging. The position is not supported by the evidence."}},"red_flags":[{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A12"],"reason":"Claims that hlb-junior 'indicates a medical necessity'. The excerpt describes school, hobby, concert and mowing uses only."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A14"],"reason":"Labels bogoch-2005-appearance as supporting T2 while admitting it does not show medical necessity. It also recasts respondents' lack of concern about appearance as products 'perceived as non-apparent'."},{"kind":"unsupported_inference","event_ids":["A18","A20"],"reason":"Favours T1 as 'likely' and recommends a launch through an existing consumer distribution network with no supporting source. All T1 assessments were neutral, and the update called T1 unsupported."},{"kind":"non_discriminating_test","event_ids":["A17"],"reason":"The 'medical' frame tests legal-standards messaging, not T2's mechanism. The decision rule is garbled and truncated. A stated willingness-to-pay difference between frames cannot establish whether either proposition justifies investment."},{"kind":"unsound_revision","event_ids":["A19","A20"],"reason":"The update is labelled 'T1 kept' but declares T1 unsupported and swaps in T2's claim. It stretches simulated wear-frequency evidence beyond T1's actual predictions. The final recommendation then ignores the update entirely."}],"suitable_for_training":false,"screen_reason":"The episode is complete but contains material unsupported inferences (A12, A14, A18/A20), a non-discriminating and truncated test (A17), and an internally contradictory update that the recommendation ignores (A19, A20). Any one of these is a critical error. Scores reflect automated evaluation only, not commercial correctness."}
